Prompt:

【Series Name】: {e.g. FOCAL IDENTITY}  
【Main Title】: {e.g. MOTION / OBSERVE / SIGNAL / FRAME}  
【Subject】: {age, gender, identity, profession}  
【Name】: {name}  
【Role】: {profession / creative role}  
【Key Line】: {short concept-driven sentence}  
【Accent Color】: {yellow / red / cobalt blue / rust orange}  
【Focus Method】: {horizontal sharp slice / floating focus window / vertical sharp slice / offset focus frame}  
【Aspect Ratio】: 9:16

Create a sophisticated editorial portrait poster built around selective focus. Use a realistic photographic portrait with natural skin texture, pores, subtle imperfections, believable hair, and authentic facial structure. Avoid plastic skin, CGI smoothness, heavy beauty retouching, or generic commercial-model styling.

Keep most of the portrait softly blurred, slightly motion-softened, desaturated, or reduced in detail. Only the selected 【Focus Method】 should return to sharp detail, natural skin tone, and clear texture. The sharp and blurred areas must remain perfectly aligned as the same person and the same photographic moment, with no duplicated eyes, second face, or pasted-photo effect.

Choose one focus structure: a horizontal sharp band across the eyes, a small floating window isolating part of the face, a narrow vertical sharp slice through motion blur, or a larger offset frame that deliberately shifts attention away from the facial center.

Use a restrained neutral palette of charcoal, gray, warm beige, off-white, and natural skin tones. Use 【Accent Color】 as the only strong accent for the focus lines, main title, and a few key details. Keep the visual rule: Neutral Field + One Accent Color.

Use mature magazine-style typography: a high-contrast editorial serif for 【Main Title】, a condensed bold sans serif for 【Name】 and 【Role】, and a small neutral sans serif for secondary information. Include 【Key Line】 as meaningful copy connected to the subject and concept, not random filler text.

Keep the composition asymmetric with generous negative space. Let the portrait, typography, and focus geometry interact naturally. Add subtle film grain, light scan texture, and restrained print texture so the whole poster feels like one finished editorial piece.

The final result should feel like a polished independent fashion, photography, music, or culture magazine poster, with blur and sharpness deliberately controlling where the viewer looks.

Avoid cyberpunk HUDs, excessive UI graphics, neon gradients, gibberish text, logos, watermarks, numbering, sparkle symbols, harsh Gaussian blur, melted facial features, duplicated features, plastic skin, and over-retouching.

